# Search

The search input feeds a parser, the parser produces a [`ParsedSearchQuery`](/api-reference/searchParser/interfaces/ParsedSearchQuery), and the data source decides how to apply it. Two parsers ship with the library; both implement [`ISearchParser`](/api-reference/searchParser/interfaces/ISearchParser).

## Basic text search

Default. The whole input becomes `searchText`; the data source matches it as a substring against the columns it considers searchable.

```typescript
import { BasicTextSearchParser } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable/searchParser';

const config: DataTableConfig<UserData> = {
    // ...
    searchParser: new BasicTextSearchParser(), // implicit default — no need to set explicitly
    enableSearch: true,
    searchDebounceMs: 200
};

const dataSource = new LocalDataSource(rows, {
    filtering: { textSearchColumns: ['userName', 'mailAddress'] }
});
```

| Option              | Type      | Description                                                                                          | Default |
|:--------------------|:----------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:--------|
| `enableSearch`      | `boolean` | Show the search textbox.                                                                             | `true`  |
| `searchDebounceMs`  | `number`  | Delay before the input is committed as a query. Higher = fewer requests + more input lag.            | `200`   |
| `forcedSearchQuery` | `ForcedSearchQuery<Data>` | Override the user's query. Useful for permanent filters or external "scope" pickers.       | —       |

## Advanced search — typed filters

`AdvancedSearchParser` understands three token shapes:

- **Free text** — anything not matching a filter or category. Falls into `searchText`.
- **Filters** — `key:value` tokens. Land in `searchFilters: { type, value }[]`.
- **Categories** — `#name` tokens (only when `enableCategoryParsing` is on). Land in `searchCategories`.

Aliases let users type whichever shorthand they prefer; all map back to one canonical type.

```typescript
import { AdvancedSearchParser } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable/searchParser';

type FilterType = 'gender' | 'test' | 'idAbove';

const searchParser = new AdvancedSearchParser<string, FilterType>({
    searchFilterAliases: {
        gender:  ['g', 'sex'],
        test:    ['t', 'tester', 'isTestUser'],
        idAbove: ['id', 'min']
    },
    enableCategoryParsing: false,
    additionalQueryPartParsers: []
});
```

| Constructor option            | Type                           | Description                                                                                  | Default |
|:------------------------------|:-------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:--------|
| `searchFilterAliases`         | `Record<Type, string[]>`       | Map filter type → accepted aliases. The type itself is also accepted.                        | `{}`    |
| `searchCategoryAliases`       | `Record<Category, string[]>`   | Same for `#category` tokens.                                                                 | `{}`    |
| `enableFilterParsing`         | `boolean`                      | Treat `key:value` tokens as filters.                                                         | `true`  |
| `enableCategoryParsing`       | `boolean`                      | Treat `#name` tokens as categories.                                                          | `true`  |
| `additionalQueryPartParsers`  | `QueryPartParser[]`            | Custom parsers tried on each unrecognised token before it falls through to `searchText`.     | `[]`    |

Aliases are matched by `startsWith`, so `g:` resolves to `gender` even if the user only types one character. Multiple definitions matching the same prefix throw a [`SearchError`](/api-reference/searchParser/classes/SearchError).

## Applying filters

The parser doesn't know what your filters mean — interpret them where the data lives.

### Client-side (LocalDataSource)

```typescript
const dataSource = new LocalDataSource(rows, {
    filtering: {
        textSearchColumns: ['userName', 'firstName', 'lastName', 'mailAddress'],
        filterFunction: (item, query) => {
            for (const filter of query.searchFilters) {
                const value = filter.value.trim().toLowerCase();
                switch (filter.type) {
                    case 'gender':
                        if (item.gender.toLowerCase() !== value) return false;
                        break;
                    case 'idAbove': {
                        const min = Number.parseInt(value, 10);
                        if (Number.isFinite(min) && item.id < min) return false;
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }
            return true;
        }
    }
});
```

### Server-side

Forward `query.searchText`, `query.searchFilters` and `query.searchCategories` in the request payload — the data source ships the full `ParsedSearchQuery` as part of the [`PaginatedListRequest`](/api-reference/index/interfaces/PaginatedListRequest), so the backend already receives them.

## Custom query-part parsers

Recognise bare tokens (no `key:` prefix) and turn them into filters or categories. Useful for natural patterns like `>100`, `2024-*`, or `@username`.

```typescript
const searchParser = new AdvancedSearchParser({
    additionalQueryPartParsers: [
        (queryPart) => {
            if (queryPart.startsWith('@')) {
                return { searchFilter: { type: 'username', value: queryPart.slice(1) } };
            }
            return false;
        }
    ]
});
```

Return `false` to fall through to the next parser. The token becomes `searchText` if no parser claims it.

